/* easter — the date of Easter Sunday, checked against a table nobody on this project wrote.

   Every year from 1600 to 2099 is worked out here and compared with the dates in
   `examples/assets/easter-dates.txt`, which the US Census Bureau publishes
   (census.gov, "Date of Easter", 1600–2099 — taken there from a listing by
   M. Bednarek and verified against M. Montes's tables). Two backends agreeing proves
   they agree; a published table is what makes agreement mean correct.

       cufet examples/algorithms/easter.cufe

   The exit contract:

       0   every year agreed with the table
       1   at least one year disagreed
       2   the table could not be read

   ── The method ──

   The anonymous Gregorian algorithm, as Meeus, Jones and Butcher give it: a chain of
   whole-number divisions and remainders that finds the paschal full moon (the epact)
   and then the Sunday after it. There is no integer division in Cufet, so each
   quotient is `math's floor` of an exact one — and the whole chain runs through two
   independent decimal implementations, one per backend, with nothing to round.

   ★ Easter falls between March 22 and April 25. The ends of that range are rare,
   which makes them a good test: the dates either side are easy to get by accident.
*/

Pull a book on math.
    /// The whole part of `top / bottom` — what the method writes as integer division.
    Bind number to whole-part, given (the number top, the number bottom):
        Return math's floor of (top / bottom).
    Done.

    /// Easter Sunday of a Gregorian year, as the table writes it: "April 20".
    Bind text to easter-of, given (the number year):
        Define golden as year % 19.
        Define century as cast whole-part on (year, 100).
        Define of-century as year % 100.
        Define leap-centuries as cast whole-part on (century, 4).
        Define century-rest as century % 4.
        Define moon-shift as cast whole-part on (century + 8, 25).
        Define moon-fix as cast whole-part on (century - moon-shift + 1, 3).
        Define epact as (19 * golden + century - leap-centuries - moon-fix + 15) % 30.
        Define leap-years as cast whole-part on (of-century, 4).
        Define year-rest as of-century % 4.
        Define to-sunday as (32 + 2 * century-rest + 2 * leap-years - epact - year-rest) % 7.
        Define correction as cast whole-part on (golden + 11 * epact + 22 * to-sunday, 451).
        Define counted as epact + to-sunday - 7 * correction + 114.
        Define month as cast whole-part on (counted, 31).
        Define day as counted % 31 + 1.
        Return "{"March" when month is 3, otherwise "April"} {day}".
    Done.

    Define table as "examples/assets/easter-dates.txt".
    Try to:
        Define lines as read all lines from the file table.
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        State "easter: {table}: {the message of the failure}".
        Exit with 2.
    Done.

    Define checked as 0.
    Define disagreements as 0.
    Define earliest as a series of number.
    Define latest as a series of number.
    For each line in lines, repeat:
        If the length of line is 0, skip.
        Define parts as line split by " ".
        Define year as (item 1 of parts) converted to number but void is 0.
        Define published as "{item 2 of parts} {item 3 of parts}".
        Define ours as cast easter-of on (year).
        Increment checked by 1.
        If ours is not published:
            Increment disagreements by 1.
            State "  {year}: we say {ours}, the table says {published}".
        Done.
        If ours is "March 22", insert year into earliest.
        If ours is "April 25", insert year into latest.
    Done.

    State "worked out {checked} years and compared each with the published table: {disagreements} disagreed".
    State "the earliest Easter, March 22, fell in {earliest}".
    State "the latest, April 25, fell in {latest}".
    If disagreements is greater than 0, exit with 1.
Done.
